<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!--
  org.ruyaos.Input1 — Ruya Input: controllers, capabilities, routing, profiles (DRAFT).

  DRAFT (T-366 / RUY-2, lead 2026-11-12), partly built. ruya-inputd serves the Devices members
  ListDevices, DeviceAdded, DeviceChanged, DeviceRemoved and the Routing members SetRoute, Route,
  RouteChanged since RUY-3 (T-367), UserActive since RUY-588, GetCapabilities and the button test (StartTest, StopTest,
  TestInput, TestEnded) since RUY-39 (T-368), and the controller profile members (ListProfiles,
  GetProfile, SaveProfile, ValidateProfile, DeleteProfile, AssignProfile for the global scope,
  GetEffectiveProfile, ProfilesChanged) since RUY-42 (T-369), SetConfirmButton, ConfirmButton and
  ActiveInputChanged since RUY-40 (per controller, with GetConfirmButtons, since RUY-603), and the
  players (JoinPlayer, LeavePlayer, ListPlayers, PlayersChanged; the device record's slot and
  profile) since RUY-121 (ADR-017 seats; the seat gate is off until RUY-122, see Players), and who
  drives the UI (ForeignInput, SetNavigator; 06 §11.3) since RUY-38; every other member below is
  still DRAFT and not served (platform and game assignments T-370 / RUY-43, OwnershipMode T-371).
  The design is docs/specs/06-input.md. Each task may change what it builds here (contracts are
  drafts until the first release, root CLAUDE.md); RUY-403's served-vs-XML test must check only
  the built members.

  Served by ruya-inputd (system user ruya-input, not root: 06 §3) on the system bus at
  /org/ruyaos/Input1. Bus policy: ruya-input owns the name; only ruya-shell and root may call.
  Internal (tier A, ADR-022): apps never call it. They see input only as evdev virtual devices of
  their own profile (docs/developers/controller-input.md) and, later, semantic actions through
  Platform1 (T-374).

  Trust: the `profile` argument of SetRoute is trusted (the shell passes the active profile, as with
  Session1.Launch); the service refuses accounts that are not profile accounts ("unknown-profile").
  When the caller that set the current route leaves the bus, the route falls back to "locked".

  Status strings are stable API:
    "ok"               success
    "unknown-device"   no connected device with that id
    "unknown-profile"  no such console profile (SetRoute, and every profile member: the console
                       profile must be a profile account, as for SetRoute), or no such controller
                       profile
    "unknown-app"      not an installed app id (assignments, ownership modes)
    "invalid"          malformed argument, or a controller profile the schema or the service's rules
                       refuse (errors says why), or a parent (`inherits`) that does not exist
    "not-allowed"      the action does not fit the current state (a button test under the route "app"
                       or "locked", saving over or deleting a built-in profile, saving a profile of scope
                       "builtin", a cycle in `inherits`, a chain of more than 8 `inherits` links,
                       more than 64 stored profiles of one console profile, JoinPlayer or LeavePlayer
                       with nobody signed in, LeavePlayer of seat 1, SetNavigator with nobody
                       signed in)
    "busy"             a button test already runs for that device
    "full"             JoinPlayer: every seat (4) is taken (RUY-121)
    "failed"           internal error (also: the profiles could not be read or written)

  Records are a{sv} so fields can be added later (unknown keys are ignored).

  Device record (a{sv}), keys:
    id          s   stable id, "<bus>-<vid>-<pid>-<key>" (06 §4.1; never an event node, no raw MAC)
    instance    s   "<id>#<n>", one per connection
    name        s   the driver's name ("DualSense Wireless Controller")
    family      s   "xbox" | "playstation" | "nintendo" | "generic"
    connection  s   "usb" | "bluetooth" | "receiver" | "virtual"
    model       s   the controller model whose silhouette the shell draws: the file stem of a drawing in
                    shell/art/pads/ ("dualsense", "wiiu_pro", ...), "" when the table does not know the
                    pad (the shell then draws the family's). From the USB ids and the driver's name;
                    the same table as Devices1's `model` (services/ruya-pads; RUY-491; a missing key
                    is "")
    layout      s   how the Button test draws the pad: "nintendo-wiiu" (Wii U Pro, 057e:0330), or ""
                    for the family's default drawing (RUY-486; a missing key is "")
    battery     i   0..100, or -1 when unknown or not on battery
    charging    b
    slot        u   the seat the pad holds (ADR-017, 1..4; the player record's slot), 0 when it is
                    not seated (RUY-121). DeviceChanged is sent when it changes.
    profile     s   the profile seated on the pad, "" when it is not seated (RUY-121; also every
                    pad while nobody is signed in). DeviceChanged is sent when it changes.
    routed      b   the pad has a virtual pad for that profile and its node's ACL is set (after
                    udev's last event on it): an app of the profile can open it now

  Capability (a(sbbbb)): name, hardware, driver, available, output (06 §4.3). Names:
    buttons sticks analog_triggers dpad gyro accelerometer touchpad rumble trigger_rumble
    adaptive_triggers lightbar player_leds speaker microphone battery
  GetCapabilities lists every name, in this order, as found when the device connected; hardware
  false means the model does not have it. Nothing is inferred (rumble never implies
  adaptive_triggers); a missing node is neither driver nor available (services/ruya-inputd/src/caps.rs).

  Routes: "shell" | "app" | "overlay" | "locked" (06 §5.1). Ownership modes: "managed" |
  "passthrough" | "steam" | "semantic" (06 §5.3).

  Controller profiles are JSON text, schema docs/specs/schemas/input-profile-v1.json (06 §7; the
  format is public, docs/developers/controller-input.md). The service validates every profile on
  load, save and import: the schema, plus the rules it cannot express (64 KiB, macro limits, button
  and axis names, dead zones that leave a range, no self-inheritance, plain-text names;
  services/ruya-inputd/src/profile.rs). Errors are "path: message" lines ("/mappings/0/to/code:
  "jump" is not a button"); they are for the journal and never shown to the player as they are.
  Built-in profiles: "default" (every input to itself, the default stick settings) and "nintendo"
  (south and east, west and north swapped; inherits "default"). A profile without inherits
  inherits from "default". Stored per console profile in /var/lib/ruya-input/profiles/<profile>/
  (0700, owned by ruya-input).
  Profile summary (a{sv}), keys:
    id                  s   the profile id
    name                s
    scope               s   "builtin" | "global" | "platform" | "game"
    scope_id            s   the app id for platform and game, else ""
    inherits            s   its parent as the file says ("" means "default")
    builtin             b
    assigned            b   the console profile's global assignment of its global_slot names it
    global_slot         s   the AssignProfile "global" scope_id its match names (RUY-545): "device:<id>"
                            when it has match.device, else "family:<f>" with match.family, else ""
                            (all controllers; every built-in)
    match_family        s   its own match.family, "" when none
    match_device        s   its own match.device (a stable device id), "" when none
    layout              s   "nintendo" when its chain holds the built-in "nintendo", else "standard"
    remapped            u   buttons it maps differently from its parent (the "changed" count of a
                            remap list on top of a layout)
    left_stick_changed  b   the resolved left stick settings differ from the built-in default's
    right_stick_changed b   the same for the right stick
    changed             b   the resolved profile maps or sets anything differently from "default"
  ListProfiles lists the built-ins first, then the stored profiles by id.
-->
<node>
  <interface name="org.ruyaos.Input1">

    <!-- Devices (T-367 built: ListDevices and the three signals; GetCapabilities built, T-368).
         Only physical pads are listed, never the service's own virtual devices. -->
    <method name="ListDevices">
      <arg name="devices" type="aa{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="GetCapabilities">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="capabilities" type="a(sbbbb)" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <signal name="DeviceAdded">
      <arg name="device" type="a{sv}"/>
    </signal>
    <!-- Battery, slot, owner or capabilities changed. -->
    <signal name="DeviceChanged">
      <arg name="device" type="a{sv}"/>
    </signal>
    <signal name="DeviceRemoved">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s"/>
      <arg name="instance" type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- Live button test (T-368, built). StartTest: "ok", "unknown-device", "not-allowed" (the
         route is "app" or "locked"), "busy" (a test already runs for that device). StopTest: "ok"
         (also when no test runs), "unknown-device". A test ends by StopTest, by the route "app" or
         "locked", when its pad disconnects, when its caller leaves the bus, or 120 s after the
         pad's last input; each end is one TestEnded. ("overlay" is allowed since RUY-611: with a
         frozen or closing game behind Home the shell's route is "overlay" with that game's
         instance, so its pads stay, and the Controllers pages must still run their test.)
         TestInput, while a test runs, for every input of that pad:
           input   the normalised name (06 §4.2: south east west north l1 r1 select start guide
                   l3 r3 dpad_up dpad_down dpad_left dpad_right, left_x left_y right_x right_y
                   left_trigger right_trigger), or, for an input without a normal name, its
                   raw source
           value   a button 1 (pressed) or 0; an axis its normalised value (sticks -32768..32767,
                   up negative; triggers 0..32767); a raw input the kernel's value
           source  the raw source, "raw:<node>:<type>:<code>" ("raw:pad:1:304"; node "pad" is the
                   pad's main event node)
         Buttons are sent at once; each axis at most 30 times a second, its newest value always
         arriving (held back at most 1/30 s).
         TestEnded reason: "stopped" | "idle" | "route" | "gone" | "caller".
         StartTest hold_left_stick (RUY-479): true holds that pad's left stick out of navigation
         while this test runs: its arrow keys are not sent (one already down is released at
         once), TestInput still reports it; the D-pad and the buttons keep navigating. When the
         test ends with the stick pushed it sends no key until it has been near neutral. false
         changes nothing. -->
    <method name="StartTest">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="hold_left_stick" type="b" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="StopTest">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <signal name="TestInput">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s"/>
      <arg name="input" type="s"/>
      <arg name="value" type="i"/>
      <arg name="source" type="s"/>
    </signal>
    <signal name="TestEnded">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s"/>
      <arg name="reason" type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- Routing (T-367, built). The shell calls it on every focus, overlay, sign-in, switch, lock
         and freeze change (06 §5.1-5.2; RUY-409, shell/qml/InputRoute.qml), and again whenever the
         name gets a new owner (a restarted service starts in "shell").
           route     "shell" | "app" | "overlay" | "locked"
           instance  Session1's instance of the app in front for "app" and "overlay" (opaque: never
                     parsed), else ""; for "overlay" also while that app is frozen (the Hub's pause,
                     RUY-541), so a Freeze by the Hub keeps the app's pads; also with no app in front
                     (RUY-611): the shell sends "overlay" with the instance of a game that is frozen
                     behind Home or the Hub, and of a game being closed until it has exited (it saves
                     its controller choice on quit), so its pads outlive the Close. "overlay" with ""
                     (an overlay that names no app; the shell no longer sends it) destroys them
           app_id    the Flatpak id of that app (the service picks the game or platform profile by
                     it, RUY-43), "" exactly when instance is ""; only its form is checked (the
                     rule of Session1: three dot-separated parts or more), it is not looked up
           profile   the active console profile id ("p1"), "" only for "shell" before a sign-in
                     and always for "locked"
         Status: "ok", "invalid" (unknown route, a malformed or missing instance or app_id, a
         profile for "locked"), "unknown-profile" (not a profile account: root, system users and
         ruya-shell never are). Virtual pads exist only for "app", and stay for "overlay" with the
         same instance and profile, frozen or not, and for the "app" that follows it: the same
         devices (event nodes), held in neutral (one neutral frame when the overlay starts, then
         nothing is written until the route is "app" again, so a frozen app finds no backlog at
         its Thaw; a button held at that "app" is masked until it is let go). Anything else
         destroys them ("locked", "shell", another instance or profile, "overlay" with ""), so
         an app's open descriptor reads ENODEV and a pad never outlives its profile. Only a seated
         pad has a virtual pad, and its number is its seat (phys ruya-input/pad<slot>; Players
         below, RUY-121; until RUY-122 the seat gate is off and unseated pads follow as pad5 and up),
         and it stays through its seat's 60 s hold when its pad disconnects (RUY-627). On "app" for a new owner pad1 (seat 1's pad, the profile's active
         controller) is made first and the other seated pads only once pad1's ACL is set (or its
         2 s udev fallback passed), so a game that opens the first pad it is offered gets pad1.
         A profile change (sign-in, switch, lock) seats the new profile in seat 1 and empties the
         others (Players). When the caller that set the route leaves the bus, the route
         becomes "locked". At start the route is "shell" with no profile. -->
    <method name="SetRoute">
      <arg name="route" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="instance" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="app_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <property name="Route" type="s" access="read"/>
    <signal name="RouteChanged">
      <arg name="route" type="s"/>
      <arg name="instance" type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- Someone is playing (RUY-588). While the route is "app" the pads' input goes to the game's
         virtual pads and the shell sees none of it, so its idle clock would run on during play.
         ruya-inputd sends this when a pad press, or a stick or trigger moved past a small threshold
         (a quarter of a stick's range, a tenth of a trigger's), reached the app route (any pad, also one that is not the profile's). It
         carries nothing (no device, no button: it is not a key logger) and is sent at most once
         per 30 s, so the bus stays quiet; the idle clock counts in minutes. In the other routes
         pad input arrives as navigation keys, which the shell counts itself, so it is not sent.
         The payload is empty, so whoever can match the broadcast learns only that someone plays. The shell
         ignores it while the screen saver or the dark screen is up: the pad's press wakes it as a
         key does (the saver puts the route on "overlay", so the game does not get that press). -->
    <signal name="UserActive"/>

    <!-- Players (DRAFT, served since RUY-121; ADR-017 points 1, 4 and 6, 06 §11.3). While a profile
         is signed in (the route's profile is not "") the session has 4 seats; a seat is one profile
         on one pad. Seat 1 is player 1: the signed-in profile, on the pad that signed it in (the
         profile's active controller, 06 §5.1 "Numbering"), or on no pad ("") after a keyboard or
         touch sign-in. Seats 2-4 start empty. Any profile change (a sign-in, a switch, Lock, the
         route's caller leaving the bus) empties every seat and seats the new profile, if any, in
         seat 1: a joined player stays for the session. A pad holds at most one profile, and a
         profile at most one pad. Only seated pads get a virtual pad, and pad n is seat n (phys
         ruya-input/pad<n>); an unseated pad's input reaches no app; who navigates the shell follows
         the seats (UI ownership below, RUY-38). A seated pad that disconnects holds its seat for 60 s
         (RUY-627, seat 1 too): the record keeps its device_id with held true, and its virtual pad
         stays (one neutral frame, then nothing; its rumble is dropped). The same stable id back
         within the hold, or the one like pad (a serial-less USB pad of the same vid:pid as exactly
         one held seat's pad, which then takes the new id; 06 §4.1), is the seat's pad again, held
         false, and its virtual pad is bound to it: no virtual device is made or destroyed, so the
         app sees no pad go or come. When the hold runs out seat n >= 2 is freed and its virtual pad
         destroyed; seat 1 keeps its profile with device_id "" and pad1 is destroyed. A held seat
         keeps its number (unseated pads never take 1-4).
         The seat gate is OFF until RUY-122 (the prompt that calls JoinPlayer; ruya-inputd's
         SEAT_GATE): meanwhile an unseated connected pad still gets a virtual pad in "app" (and what
         "overlay" keeps), numbered after the seats (ruya-input/pad5 and up, the lowest free number,
         in connect order), so pad1..pad4 stay the seats' and a join renumbers no other live pad.
         Also while the gate is off (RUY-38 round 2, the interim fallback): when seat 1 has no pad
         and is not held (a keyboard sign-in, or its pad's hold ran out) and no navigator is set, the
         first unseated pad that presses a button becomes seat 1's pad (PlayersChanged, its virtual
         pad is pad1) and navigates from that press on; with the gate on (RUY-122) only JoinPlayer
         (the prompt's Move) seats player 1 on a pad. Seats, ListPlayers and every member here work
         the same either way otherwise.
         A restarted service knows no seats: the shell joins the players it last knew again on the
         name's new owner (seat 1's pad as the route's profile, then the others by slot), each once
         the service lists its pad (a held seat's player only when its pad is back).
         The service never sees a PIN: the shell checks it (Profiles1) before it calls JoinPlayer.
         The journal names ids only: "player <n> is ruya-<profile> on <device id>" ("on no pad" for
         seat 1 without one), "player <n> left", and for the hold "player <n> (ruya-<profile>) held
         for <s> s: <id> is gone", "player <n> (ruya-<profile>) is back on <id>" and "player <n>'s
         hold ran out: <id> did not come back".

         JoinPlayer(device_id, profile): seats `profile` on the connected pad `device_id`.
           - profile is the signed-in profile: the pad becomes seat 1's (player 1's active
             controller); the pad seat 1 had is unseated.
           - profile sits in seat n (2-4) on another pad: it moves to this pad and keeps seat n;
             the old pad is unseated.
           - the pad already sits as another profile: that profile leaves its seat first (seat 1
             keeps its profile and has no pad then).
           - otherwise the profile takes the lowest free seat of 2-4.
           - the same pad and profile again: "ok" with its seat, nothing changes, no signal.
           A move binds the seat's virtual pad to the new pad (RUY-627: the same device, no virtual
           pad made or destroyed; a pad that joins under "overlay" without one gets it when the app
           is in front again); a move of a held seat ends its hold. Leaving destroys it. Live
           virtual pads are never renumbered.
           Status (checked in this order): "invalid" (not a device id: [a-z0-9:-], 1..64),
           "not-allowed" (nobody is signed in: route "locked", or "shell" with profile ""),
           "unknown-profile" (not a profile account, the SetRoute rule), "unknown-device" (no
           connected pad with that id), "full" (no free seat; nothing changes), "ok". slot: the seat
           taken (1..4), 0 unless "ok".
         LeavePlayer(slot): empties seat `slot` (its pad is unseated, its virtual pad destroyed).
           Status: "invalid" (0 or more than 4), "not-allowed" (nobody signed in, or seat 1), "ok"
           (also for a seat that is already empty: nothing changes, no signal).
         ListPlayers: the seated players, by slot. PlayersChanged carries the whole list, once per
         change (Join, Leave, a profile change, a hold that starts or ends; never once a second for
         held_secs: the shell counts down itself).
         Player record (a{sv}), keys:
           slot       u   1..4
           profile    s   the console profile id
           device_id  s   the pad's stable id (the device record's id), "" when the seat has no pad
                          (seat 1 after a keyboard sign-in or after its pad's hold ran out); while
                          the seat is held, the id of the pad that is gone
           held       b   the seat's pad is gone and the seat waits for it (the 60 s hold, RUY-627)
           held_secs  u   the whole seconds the hold has left when the record was sent (rounded up),
                          0 when the seat is not held -->
    <method name="JoinPlayer">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="slot" type="u" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="LeavePlayer">
      <arg name="slot" type="u" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="ListPlayers">
      <arg name="players" type="aa{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <signal name="PlayersChanged">
      <arg name="players" type="aa{sv}"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- UI ownership and the active input (06 §11.3 and §11.4, ADR-017 points 2 and 9; RUY-38).
         Who navigates the shell (the navigation keys on "RuyaOS Navigation", Guide as KEY_HOMEPAGE
         included; the keyboard always does, the service does not see it):
           - nobody signed in (route "locked", or "shell" with profile ""): every pad, and Guide from
             any pad is KEY_HOMEPAGE (the picker, its PIN pad, first boot);
           - signed in, no navigator: only seat 1's pad (Players). Seats 2-4 give the shell nothing
             (under "app" their input goes to their virtual pad); an unseated pad gives the shell
             nothing either (and no app, once the seat gate is on). With seat 1 held or without a
             pad no pad navigates;
           - a navigator set (SetNavigator): only that pad, every key including Guide; seat 1's pad
             gives nothing, Guide included. The other pads behave as above, ForeignInput included.
         Under "app" a pad that navigates gives the shell its Guide only, as before. Keys held by a
         pad that stops navigating (the navigator set or cleared, a move of player 1) are released,
         so no key sticks. UserActive does not depend on this.

         ForeignInput(device_id, kind): a pad that does not navigate now asked for something while a
         profile is signed in. kind:
           "press"         an unseated pad pressed a button other than Guide (never an axis, stick
                           drift or a release), at most once per pad per second;
           "guide"         an unseated pad pressed Guide;
           "player-guide"  a seated pad of seat 2-4 pressed Guide.
         Seat 1's pad sends none (also not while a navigator is set). Never sent while nobody is
         signed in (every pad navigates then). -->
    <signal name="ForeignInput">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s"/>
      <arg name="kind" type="s"/>
    </signal>
    <!-- SetNavigator(device_id): while it is set, only the connected pad device_id navigates the
         shell (above): the pad of "Who's playing?" (RUY-122) drives the prompt. "" clears it. The
         service announces that pad as the active input at the call (ActiveInputChanged, and
         ConfirmButton follows it), so the hint bar shows its glyphs and its own Confirm choice.
         It is cleared by "", by that pad's removal, by any change of the route's profile and when
         the caller that set it leaves the bus (as the route is). A restarted service has none: the
         shell sets it again on the name's new owner, as it joins the players again.
         Status: "ok" ("" is always "ok"), "not-allowed" (nobody signed in), "unknown-device" (no
         connected pad with that id). Journal: "navigator <id>", "navigator cleared (<why>)". -->
    <method name="SetNavigator">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- The device of the last press among the pads that navigate now (above; 06 §11.4), or the
         navigator at SetNavigator; family as in the device record. Sent when the device differs
         from the last announced one (at SetNavigator always), not on every press. The keyboard is
         the shell's own knowledge, not reported here. -->
    <signal name="ActiveInputChanged">
      <arg name="device_id" type="s"/>
      <arg name="family" type="s"/>
    </signal>
    <!-- mode: "family" (as on the controller, default) | "south" | "east" (06 §11.2). Per console
         profile (`profile`, which must be a profile account) and per controller (`device`, the
         pad's stable id of the device record, as Controls in games uses it; the pad need not be
         connected) (RUY-603), kept in ruya-input's state directory and applied from that pad's next
         press on; a button held now is released as it was pressed. "family" removes the pad's
         choice (a pad with none is "family"). It changes the shell's navigation keys only (accept
         and back); the virtual pads stay positional. Status: "ok", "invalid" (not one of the
         modes, or not a device id), "unknown-profile" (not a profile account), "failed" (not
         written; nothing changed). -->
    <method name="SetConfirmButton">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="device" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="mode" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- The console profile's choices, device id -> mode ("south" | "east"; a pad with no choice
         is absent and is "family"), for the controller pages (RUY-603). Status: "ok",
         "unknown-profile" (not a profile account), "failed" (not read; buttons empty). -->
    <method name="GetConfirmButtons">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="buttons" type="a{ss}" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- ConfirmButton: the mode of the active controller (the pad of the last press, as
         ActiveInputChanged reports it) for the signed-in profile (the route's); "family" with no
         profile, no active pad or no choice for that pad. PropertiesChanged is sent when the value
         may have changed: on SetConfirmButton for the active pad, on a profile switch, and when
         another pad becomes the active one (or the active pad goes) with another mode. The
         shell's accept and back glyphs follow it (RUY-41). -->
    <property name="ConfirmButton" type="s" access="read"/>

    <!-- Controller profiles (T-369 built; platform and game assignments T-370), stored per console
         profile (`profile`, which must be a profile account, else "unknown-profile"). Every change
         (save, delete, assign) is one ProfilesChanged and reaches the virtual pads of that console
         profile at once: what each pad emits is the global profile that applies to it (RUY-545,
         06 §7): the one assigned to its device's slot, else its family's, else all controllers',
         else "default". A held button is released and its new target pressed. -->
    <method name="ListProfiles">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="profiles" type="aa{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="GetProfile">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="profile_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="json" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- GetProfile: the profile as stored (only what it sets; a built-in as the console has it);
         "unknown-profile" for an id that is neither.
         SaveProfile creates or replaces (same id). Import is ValidateProfile, then SaveProfile.
         Status "ok" (profile_id is the id), "invalid" (errors say why: the file, or a parent that
         does not exist), "not-allowed" (a built-in id or scope, a cycle, more than 8 links for it or
         for a profile that inherits from it, a 65th profile), "failed" (not written; nothing
         changed). The stored text is the service's own writing of the profile (keys sorted, whole
         numbers without a fraction), not the caller's text.
         The "controls-" id prefix is reserved for global profiles (the shell's slot ids
         controls-all, controls-family-<f>, controls-device-<id>): a profile with that prefix and
         another scope is "invalid" (error "/id: ..."), in ValidateProfile and in SaveProfile. -->
    <method name="SaveProfile">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="json" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="profile_id" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="errors" type="as" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- The file alone: "ok", or "invalid" with errors; its parent is checked by SaveProfile.
         Same rules as SaveProfile, including the reserved "controls-" id prefix (global only). -->
    <method name="ValidateProfile">
      <arg name="json" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="errors" type="as" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- "ok", "unknown-profile", "not-allowed" (a built-in). Its children inherit from its parent
         afterwards and keep their overrides; a global assignment that named it is removed. -->
    <method name="DeleteProfile">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="profile_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- scope: "global" | "platform" | "game" (scope_id: the app id). Built today: "global" (RUY-545
         slots): a console profile has up to one global profile per slot, scope_id "" (all
         controllers), "family:<f>" (f one of xbox, playstation, nintendo, generic) or "device:<id>"
         (a stable device id); a pad takes its device's slot, else its family's, else "", else
         "default". The profile is a built-in or a global profile whose global_slot (its match) is
         this scope_id; "invalid" for another slot, a malformed scope_id, or a profile of scope
         platform or game; "unknown-profile" for an id that does not exist. profile_id "" empties
         that slot only ("ok" on a slot that is already empty: nothing changes, ProfilesChanged is
         still sent); assigning one slot never touches another. A SaveProfile that changes an
         assigned profile's match takes it out of its old slot. "platform" and "game" answer
         "invalid" until T-370 / RUY-43. ListProfiles' assigned and global_slot read the slots. -->
    <method name="AssignProfile">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="scope" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="scope_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="profile_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <!-- What would apply to this device in this app: the resolved profile and its chain,
         most specific first (game, platform, global, builtin). json is one schema-v1 document: the
         most specific profile's id, name, scope and match, no inherits, every mapping of the
         chain, and both sticks' settings complete (the defaults filled in). device_id "" is a pad
         that no match names; a device_id that is not connected is "unknown-device". app_id is ""
         or an app id ("invalid" otherwise); until T-370 / RUY-43 it does not change the answer. -->
    <method name="GetEffectiveProfile">
      <arg name="profile" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="device_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="app_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="json" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="chain" type="as" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <signal name="ProfilesChanged">
      <arg name="profile" type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- Ownership modes (T-371), per app; applied at the next launch. -->
    <method name="GetOwnershipMode">
      <arg name="app_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="mode" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="SetOwnershipMode">
      <arg name="app_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="mode" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

  </interface>
</node>
